    "question": {
      ":question/id": "567e6e48-79d4-578a-92ec-8e6725dfc470",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $K$ into the matrix $L$, where\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that the $1$st and the $2$nd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $3$rd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $3$rd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
